select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 15, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.0", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.0 Overview", "body": "Brady, N. F., et al. (1988). Report of the Presidential Task Force on Market Mechanisms. U.S. Government Printing Office.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 19.\nLeland, H. E. (1980). Who should buy portfolio insurance? Journal of Finance, 35(2), 581–594.\nRubinstein, M., and Leland, H. E. (1981). Replicating options with positions in stock and cash. Financial Analysts Journal, 37(4), 63–72.\nTaleb, N. N. (1997). Dynamic Hedging: Managing Vanilla and Exotic Options. Wiley."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.0", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nWhich option carries the higher implied volatility?\nA stock index stands at 6,000. Two three-month European options are traded: a put with a strike of 5,400, which pays if the index falls by more than 10 percent, and a call with a strike of 6,600, which pays if it rises by more than 10 percent. The Black–Scholes–Merton model assumes one volatility for the index and therefore assigns the same implied volatility to both. Which pattern is observed in the market?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.1", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.1", "heading": "Implied volatility across strike prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.1 Implied volatility across strike prices", "body": "Section 12.6 defined the implied volatility of an option as the volatility at which the Black–Scholes–Merton formula reproduces its market price. If the model described the market exactly, every option on one asset would have the same implied volatility, whatever its strike price and maturity, because the model assumes that the asset has one constant volatility. This is not what is observed. A plot of the implied volatilities of options with one maturity against their strike prices is called a volatility smile, from the shape that it takes in some markets. The shape differs from one market to another, and it is the subject of this chapter.\nA preliminary result simplifies the discussion. Put–call parity, Result 8.2 as extended in Chapter 13, is \\(p+S_0e^{-qT}=c+Ke^{-rT}\\). It rests on an arbitrage argument alone and holds whatever the distribution of the future price. It therefore holds for the market prices \\(p_{\\text{mkt}}\\) and \\(c_{\\text{mkt}}\\), and it holds for the prices \\(p_{\\text{BS}}\\) and \\(c_{\\text{BS}}\\) that the model produces at any one volatility. Subtracting one equation from the other,\nResult 15.1 — Calls and puts share one implied volatility"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.1", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.1", "heading": "Implied volatility across strike prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.1 Implied volatility across strike prices", "body": "\\[ p_{\\text{BS}}-p_{\\text{mkt}}=c_{\\text{BS}}-c_{\\text{mkt}} . \\] The amount by which the model misprices a European put equals the amount by which it misprices the European call with the same strike and maturity. At the volatility for which the model matches the price of the call, it matches the price of the put. The two options have the same implied volatility.\nThe smile may therefore be drawn without stating whether calls or puts were used. In practice the implied volatilities for low strikes are taken from puts and those for high strikes from calls, because options that are out of the money are the more actively traded."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.1", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.1", "heading": "Key terms", "kind": "term", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.1 Key terms", "body": "Key terms: Volatility smile: the relation between implied volatility and strike price for options with one maturity. Volatility skew: a smile in which implied volatility falls, or rises, steadily as the strike increases. At-the-money volatility: the implied volatility of the option whose strike equals the current price or the forward price of the asset. Moneyness: the strike expressed in relation to the price of the asset, commonly as the ratio \\(K/S_0\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.2", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.2", "heading": "Currency options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.2 Currency options", "body": "For options on the major currencies, implied volatility is lowest when the strike is near the forward exchange rate and rises on both sides. The smile is approximately symmetric. An option far out of the money in either direction is priced at a higher volatility than an option at the money.\nThe pattern is explained by the distribution of exchange rate changes. An option far out of the money pays only if the exchange rate moves a long way. If the market prices such options above their Black–Scholes–Merton values at the at-the-money volatility, it assigns to large movements a greater probability than the lognormal distribution does. The distribution that the market uses has heavier tails than the lognormal distribution, in both directions."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.2", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.2", "heading": "Currency options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.2 Currency options", "body": "The evidence supports the market. Under the model, daily changes in the logarithm of an exchange rate are normally distributed. Table 15.1 gives the frequency with which a normally distributed change exceeds a given number of standard deviations. Studies of exchange rates find that changes of more than three standard deviations occur on about 1 percent of days and not on 0.27 percent, and that changes of more than four or five standard deviations, which under the normal distribution should be seen once in decades or in millennia, are seen every few years.\nTable 15.1 Frequency of large daily changes under the normal distribution (changes in either direction; 252 trading days per year)\nSize of the changePercentage of daysExpected frequency\nMore than 1 standard deviation31.73One day in 3\nMore than 2 standard deviations4.55One day in 22\nMore than 3 standard deviations0.27One day in 370 (about 1.5 years)\nMore than 4 standard deviations0.0063One day in 15,800 (about 63 years)\nMore than 5 standard deviations0.000057One day in 1,740,000 (about 6,900 years)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.2", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.2", "heading": "Currency options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.2 Currency options", "body": "Two of the assumptions of the model are responsible. The volatility of an exchange rate is not constant: periods of calm alternate with periods of disturbance, and a mixture of the two produces more very small and more very large changes than one normal distribution with the average volatility. And exchange rates do not always move continuously: an announcement by a central bank may move a rate by several percent in a moment. Both effects produce heavy tails. The effect of non-constant volatility is more pronounced for long maturities and that of jumps for short maturities."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.3#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.3", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.3", "heading": "Equity options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.3 Equity options", "body": "For options on shares and on stock indices the pattern is different, and it answers the opening question. Implied volatility decreases as the strike price increases. A put with a low strike is priced at a much higher volatility than a call with a high strike. This pattern is called a volatility skew, or sometimes a smirk.\nThe corresponding distribution has a heavier left tail and a lighter right tail than the lognormal distribution with the at-the-money volatility. The market assigns to a large fall in the index a greater probability than the lognormal distribution does, and to a large rise a smaller probability. Three explanations are offered, and they are not exclusive.\nLeverage. When the shares of a company fall in value, its debt is unchanged, and the ratio of debt to equity rises. The shares become riskier, and their volatility increases. When the shares rise, the reverse occurs. Volatility is therefore a decreasing function of the share price, as the skew implies.\nVolatility feedback. An increase in the volatility of the market raises the return that investors require, and prices fall to provide it. Falls in the index and rises in volatility are therefore observed together."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.3#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.3", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.3", "heading": "Equity options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.3 Equity options", "body": "The demand for protection. Investors as a group hold shares and wish to insure them against large falls. They buy puts with low strikes. Those who sell such puts cannot hedge them reliably in a market that falls abruptly, as Section 14.10 showed, and require a premium. The term crashophobia was applied to this explanation by Mark Rubinstein.\nThe third explanation is supported by the history of the pattern, which the historical note recounts: the skew in index options has been present since the fall in share prices of October 1987 and was not present before it."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.3#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.3", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.3", "heading": "Equity options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.3 Equity options", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 15.1 — From the smile to the distribution\nThe distribution of the future price that a smile implies\nThe upper figure shows the implied volatility of three-month options against the strike, which is stated as a percentage of the current price. The lower figure shows the risk-neutral distribution of the price in three months that these volatilities imply, together with the lognormal distribution at the at-the-money volatility. Select a market, then vary the slope and the curvature of the smile and observe the tails of the distribution."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.3", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.3", "heading": "Equity options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.3 Equity options", "body": "Concept check 15.1\nIn a certain market, options with strikes far above the current price have higher implied volatilities than options at the money, and options with strikes far below have lower implied volatilities. What does this imply about the distribution that the market assigns to the future price, relative to the lognormal distribution?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.4#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.4", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.4", "heading": "The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.4 The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "body": "Implied volatility varies with maturity as well as with strike. The relation between the at-the-money implied volatility and the maturity of the option is the volatility term structure. When recent volatility has been low by historical standards, the term structure tends to slope upward, because volatility is expected to rise toward its normal level; when recent volatility has been high, it tends to slope downward. Volatility, unlike a share price, is drawn back toward a long-run average.\nThe smile and the term structure are combined in a volatility surface, which gives the implied volatility for each strike and each maturity. Table 15.2 is a surface for options on the index of the opening decision. It is illustrative, with the features that such surfaces commonly have: a skew at every maturity, steeper for short maturities than for long, and an at-the-money volatility that rises with maturity. Some entries of a surface correspond to options that are actively traded, and their implied volatilities are observed directly. The remainder are obtained by interpolation.\nTable 15.2 A volatility surface for options on a stock index at 6,000 (implied volatility, percent per year)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.4#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.4", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.4", "heading": "The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.4 The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "body": "Strike \\(K/S_0\\)1 month3 months6 months1 year2 years\n0.90 (5,400)27.924.023.323.222.9\n0.95 (5,700)21.720.821.121.721.9\n1.00 (6,000)16.818.019.320.420.9\n1.05 (6,300)13.015.717.619.220.1\n1.10 (6,600)10.213.816.218.119.3\nThe skew flattens as maturity increases when it is measured against \\(K/S_0\\), as in the table. A strike 10 percent below the index is a remote level for an option with one month to run and an ordinary level for an option with two years to run. For this reason the smile is often expressed against a measure that allows for maturity: the strike in relation to the forward price, \\(K/F_0\\); the number of standard deviations by which the strike differs from the forward price, \\(\\ln(K/F_0)/(\\sigma\\sqrt{T})\\); or the delta of the option. Smiles expressed in these ways are more nearly the same from one maturity to the next."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.4#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.4", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.4", "heading": "The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "kind": "example", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.4 The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "body": "Worked example 15.1 — Valuing an option from the surface\nA client asks for the price of a nine-month European put on the index with a strike of 5,550. The risk-free rate is 4 percent and the dividend yield on the index is 1.2 percent. Value the put from Table 15.2.\n1\nLocate the option. \\(K/S_0=5{,}550/6{,}000=0.925\\), midway between the rows for 0.90 and 0.95. Nine months is midway between the columns for six months and one year.\n2\nInterpolate across strikes. Six months: \\((23.3+21.1)/2=22.2\\) percent. One year: \\((23.2+21.7)/2=22.45\\) percent.\n3\nInterpolate across maturities. \\((22.2+22.45)/2=22.3\\) percent, to one decimal place.\n4\nApply Result 13.1. \\(\\sigma\\sqrt{T}=0.223\\times0.8660=0.1931\\). \\(d_1=[\\ln(6{,}000/5{,}550)+(0.04-0.012+0.223^{2}/2)\\times0.75]/0.1931=(0.07796+0.03965)/0.1931=0.6090\\); \\(d_2=0.4159\\). \\(N(-d_1)=0.2713\\); \\(N(-d_2)=0.3388\\). \\(p=5{,}550e^{-0.03}\\times0.3388-6{,}000e^{-0.009}\\times0.2713=1{,}824.5-1{,}613.0=211.5\\) index points.\n5"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.4", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.4", "heading": "The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "kind": "example", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.4 The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "body": "Comparison. At the nine-month at-the-money volatility, \\((19.3+20.4)/2=19.85\\) percent, the same formula gives 170.3. A dealer who ignored the skew would sell the put for about 20 percent less than the price at which it could be bought back in the market."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.4", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.4", "heading": "The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "kind": "example", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.4 The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "body": "Worked example 15.2 — The cost of ignoring the skew\nValue the two three-month options of the opening decision, first at the at-the-money volatility of 18.0 percent and then at the volatilities of Table 15.2.\n1\nPut with a strike of 5,400. At 18.0 percent the put is worth 25.8 index points. At its own implied volatility of 24.0 percent it is worth 63.7, about two and a half times as much.\n2\nCall with a strike of 6,600. At 18.0 percent the call is worth 48.5 index points. At its own implied volatility of 13.8 percent it is worth 20.5, less than half as much.\n3\nInterpretation. Differences of a few points of implied volatility correspond to large proportional differences in the prices of options far out of the money. These are the options whose prices depend most on the tails of the distribution, about which the lognormal assumption is least reliable."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.4", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.4", "heading": "The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.4 The term structure of volatility and the volatility surface", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 15.2 — Three-dimensional view\nThe volatility surface\nThe surface is that of Table 15.2, shown for strikes from 80 to 120 percent of the index and maturities from one month to two years. The gold curve is the smile at the selected maturity and the marker the selected strike. The skew is steep at the front of the figure, where maturity is short, and flattens toward the back. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.5#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.5", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.5", "heading": "Probabilities implied by option prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.5 Probabilities implied by option prices", "body": "The interpretation of the smile in terms of the tails of a distribution can be made exact. Consider two European puts with strikes slightly below and slightly above a level \\(K\\), at \\(K-\\delta\\) and \\(K+\\delta\\). A position that is long the put with the higher strike and short the put with the lower strike, a bear spread in the terms of Section 9.3, pays \\(2\\delta\\) if the price ends below \\(K-\\delta\\), nothing if it ends above \\(K+\\delta\\), and an intermediate amount between the two. When \\(\\delta\\) is small, the position is close to a contract that pays \\(2\\delta\\) if the price ends below \\(K\\) and nothing otherwise. By risk-neutral valuation the value of such a contract is \\(2\\delta e^{-rT}\\) multiplied by the risk-neutral probability of that event. Hence\nResult 15.2 — The probability implied by a spread\n\\[ \\Pr(S_T<K)\\approx e^{rT}\\,\\frac{p(K+\\delta)-p(K-\\delta)}{2\\delta}, \\] where \\(p(K)\\) is the market price of a European put with strike \\(K\\) and the probability is that of a risk-neutral world."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.5#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.5", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.5", "heading": "Probabilities implied by option prices", "kind": "example", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.5 Probabilities implied by option prices", "body": "Worked example 15.3 — The probability of a fall of 20 percent\nThree-month puts on the index with strikes of 4,750 and 4,850 trade at 28.11 and 30.79 index points. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Find the risk-neutral probability that the index is below 4,800 in three months, and compare it with the probability under a lognormal distribution with a volatility of 18.0 percent, at which the same puts would be worth 0.56 and 1.18.\n1\nFrom market prices. \\(e^{0.01}\\times(30.79-28.11)/100=1.01005\\times0.0268=0.0271\\), or 2.7 percent.\n2\nFrom the lognormal distribution. \\(1.01005\\times(1.18-0.56)/100=0.0063\\), or 0.6 percent.\n3\nInterpretation. The market prices options as though a fall of 20 percent within three months were more than four times as probable as the model, at the at-the-money volatility, indicates. The probability is a risk-neutral one. It reflects the price that investors will pay for protection against such a fall, as well as their estimate of its likelihood, and it should not be read as a forecast."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.6#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.6", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.6", "heading": "★ The implied density", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.6 ★ The implied density", "body": "Optional section. This section obtains the whole risk-neutral distribution from option prices. It uses differentiation under the integral sign.\nLet \\(g(S_T)\\) be the risk-neutral probability density of the price at time \\(T\\). By Principle 10.1 the price of a European call with strike \\(K\\) is \\[ c=e^{-rT}\\int_{K}^{\\infty}(S_T-K)\\,g(S_T)\\,dS_T . \\] Differentiating once with respect to \\(K\\) gives \\(\\partial c/\\partial K=-e^{-rT}\\int_K^\\infty g(S_T)\\,dS_T\\), which is \\(-e^{-rT}\\) multiplied by the probability that the call is exercised. Differentiating again,\nResult 15.3 — The implied density\n\\[ g(K)=e^{rT}\\frac{\\partial^{2}c}{\\partial K^{2}}\\approx e^{rT}\\,\\frac{c(K-\\delta)-2c(K)+c(K+\\delta)}{\\delta^{2}} . \\] The same expression holds with put prices in place of call prices."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.6#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.6", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.6", "heading": "★ The implied density", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.6 ★ The implied density", "body": "The numerator is the cost of a butterfly spread, Section 9.3: long one option at each of the outer strikes and short two at the middle strike. The butterfly pays a triangle of height \\(\\delta\\) centred on \\(K\\), and its price measures the probability that the price ends near \\(K\\). Since the payoff of a butterfly is never negative, its price cannot be negative, and the density that a smile implies must be positive at every strike. A smile that is too steep or too sharply curved violates this condition and would permit arbitrage. The lower panel of Interactive figure 15.1 was drawn by applying Result 15.3 at each strike.\nFor example, the three-month puts on the index with strikes of 4,750, 4,800, and 4,850 trade at 28.111, 29.372, and 30.786. The butterfly costs \\(28.111-2\\times29.372+30.786=0.153\\). The density at 4,800 is \\(1.01005\\times0.153/50^{2}=0.0000618\\) per index point, and the probability that the index ends within 25 points of 4,800 is approximately \\(0.0000618\\times50=0.31\\) percent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.7#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.7", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.7", "heading": "The role of the model", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.7 The role of the model", "body": "If the implied volatility of every option is different, in what sense is the Black–Scholes–Merton model used? The answer is that practitioners use it as a means of quotation and interpolation and not as a statement about the behaviour of the asset. The prices of actively traded options are observed. They are converted into implied volatilities, which vary far less across strikes and maturities than the prices do, and a surface is fitted to them. The price of an option that is not actively traded is then found by reading its volatility from the surface and applying the formula, as in Worked example 15.1. The procedure ensures that the option is priced consistently with those that are traded. The model serves as the instrument by which prices are compared and interpolated, and the surface carries the information that the model omits.\nThe procedure is reliable for European options, whose prices depend only on the distribution of the price at one date. It is less reliable for the exotic options of Chapter 18, whose values depend on the path of the price, and for these, models that allow the volatility to vary are required."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.7#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.7", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.7", "heading": "The role of the model", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.7 The role of the model", "body": "The smile also affects hedging. The delta of Chapter 14 assumes that the implied volatility of an option is unchanged when the price of the asset changes. For equity options this is not so: implied volatilities tend to rise when the index falls and to fall when it rises. The price of a call therefore responds to a rise in the index by less than its Black–Scholes–Merton delta indicates, because the accompanying fall in volatility offsets part of the gain. Traders allow for this by using a delta somewhat lower than \\(N(d_1)\\) for equity options. Two rules of thumb describe how the surface is assumed to move. Under the sticky strike rule the implied volatility of an option with a given strike is unchanged from day to day. Under the sticky delta rule the smile moves with the price of the asset, so that the implied volatility is unchanged for a given moneyness."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.7#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.7", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.7", "heading": "The role of the model", "kind": "warning", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.7 The role of the model", "body": "Common error\nAn option with a high implied volatility is not thereby shown to be overpriced, and the skew is not an opportunity for arbitrage. Implied volatility is the price of the option in other units. A put far out of the money has a high implied volatility because the market assigns to large falls a greater probability, or a greater price, than the lognormal distribution does. To sell such puts and hedge their delta is to sell insurance against a fall in the market, which earns a premium in most periods and incurs a very large loss in a few."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.8#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.8", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.8", "heading": "An anticipated jump", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.8 An anticipated jump", "body": "The smiles described so far are those of ordinary conditions. The method of Section 15.5 applies equally to special conditions, and one case is instructive. Suppose that a single announcement, such as the outcome of a regulatory decision or of litigation, is expected shortly and will move the price of a share sharply in one direction or the other. The distribution of the price after the announcement has two peaks and little probability between them. The smile that corresponds is the reverse of that for currencies: implied volatility is highest at the money and falls on both sides. It is sometimes called a volatility frown."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.8#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.8", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.8", "heading": "An anticipated jump", "kind": "example", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.8 An anticipated jump", "body": "Worked example 15.4 — A share awaiting an announcement\nA share trades at $50. In one month an announcement will move its price to either $58 or $42. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Value one-month European calls with strikes from $44 to $56 and find their implied volatilities.\n1\nRisk-neutral probability. By Result 10.1, \\(p=(50e^{0.04/12}-42)/(58-42)=(50.1669-42)/16=0.5104\\).\n2\nCall values. A call with a strike \\(K\\) between 42 and 58 pays \\(58-K\\) with probability 0.5104 and nothing otherwise: \\(c=e^{-0.04/12}\\times0.5104\\times(58-K)\\).\n3\nImplied volatilities. Each value is converted to an implied volatility by the method of Section 12.6.\nStrike ($)44464850525456\nCall value ($)7.126.105.094.073.052.031.02\nImplied volatility (%)58.266.469.669.566.660.850.9\n4\nInterpretation. Implied volatility is greatest near the current price and falls away on both sides. A lognormal distribution wide enough to reach $42 and $58 assigns substantial probability to prices beyond them, which the true distribution excludes; options with strikes near those levels are therefore worth less than the model indicates."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.9#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.9", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.9", "heading": "The VIX index", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.9 The VIX index", "body": "The implied volatilities of options on the S&P 500 index are summarized in one number, the VIX index, published by the Cboe. It was introduced in 1993 and has been computed since 2003 from the prices of calls and puts across a wide range of strikes, weighted so that the result measures the volatility of the index that the market expects over the following 30 days. It does not depend on the Black–Scholes–Merton model or on any single strike. The index is stated in percent per year.\nA VIX of 20 corresponds to a standard deviation of the return on the S&P 500 over the next 30 days of \\(20\\times\\sqrt{30/365}=5.7\\) percent, and to a daily standard deviation of \\(20/\\sqrt{252}=1.26\\) percent. The index has been close to 20 on average over its history. It rises sharply when share prices fall, as the skew would suggest, and for this reason it is sometimes called a gauge of fear. Its closing value exceeded 80 in November 2008 and again in March 2020. Futures and options on the VIX have been traded since 2004 and 2006, and allow volatility itself to be bought and sold.\nConcept check 15.2"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.9#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.9", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.9", "heading": "The VIX index", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.9 The VIX index", "body": "On a certain day the implied volatility of a three-month call on a share with a strike of $60 is 30 percent. A three-month put on the same share with the same strike is quoted at a price that corresponds to an implied volatility of 35 percent. Both are European and the share pays no dividend. What follows?\nConcept check 15.3\nA bank values all its three-month index options at the at-the-money implied volatility. Relative to the market, how does it value puts far out of the money and calls far out of the money?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.9#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.9", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.9", "heading": "The VIX index", "kind": "notes", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.9 The VIX index", "body": "Historical note\nBefore October 1987 the implied volatilities of options on American stock indices were nearly the same at all strikes, as the Black–Scholes–Merton model requires, and a slight symmetric smile was the most that could be detected. On 19 October 1987 the S&P 500 index fell by 20.5 percent. At a volatility of 20 percent per year the standard deviation of a daily change is 1.26 percent, so that the fall was one of about 16 standard deviations. Table 15.1 does not extend so far; under the normal distribution such an event should not occur once in the life of the universe."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.9#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.9", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.9", "heading": "The VIX index", "kind": "notes", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.9 The VIX index", "body": "Mark Rubinstein, who had been among the founders of portfolio insurance, documented what followed. From that time the prices of index puts with low strikes have been persistently higher, in relation to other options, than before, and the skew of Section 15.3 has been present in every year since. The market had learned that the distribution of index returns has a left tail that the lognormal distribution does not describe, and it has not since allowed the fact to go unpriced. In 1994 Rubinstein, and independently Emanuel Derman and Iraj Kani and Bruno Dupire, showed how a model of the price of the asset could be constructed to agree with the whole of an observed volatility surface."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.summary#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.summary", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.summary Summary", "body": "A volatility smile is the relation between implied volatility and strike for options with one maturity. By put–call parity, European calls and puts with the same strike and maturity have the same implied volatility.\nFor currency options implied volatility rises on both sides of the money, which corresponds to a distribution with heavier tails than the lognormal distribution.\nFor equity options implied volatility falls as the strike rises, which corresponds to a heavier left tail and a lighter right tail. Leverage, volatility feedback, and the demand for protection against large falls are the explanations offered.\nA volatility surface gives implied volatility by strike and maturity. Options are valued by reading the volatility from the surface and applying the Black–Scholes–Merton formula.\nThe prices of options with neighbouring strikes reveal risk-neutral probabilities: a spread gives the probability of ending below a level, and a butterfly gives the density.\nThe VIX index measures the volatility of the S&P 500 that option prices imply for the following 30 days."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.exercises Exercises", "body": "15.1 At a volatility of 25 percent the Black–Scholes–Merton prices of a European call and a European put with the same strike and maturity are $4.20 and $3.10. The call trades in the market at $4.50. At what price must the put trade, and what can be said of the implied volatilities of the two options?\nBy Result 15.1, \\(p_{\\text{mkt}}-p_{\\text{BS}}=c_{\\text{mkt}}-c_{\\text{BS}}=4.50-4.20=0.30\\), so that the put must trade at \\(3.10+0.30=\\$3.40\\). Both options are priced above their values at 25 percent, and both have the same implied volatility, which is higher than 25 percent.\n15.2 Three-month options on a share priced at $100 have implied volatilities of 22 percent at a strike of $95 and 19 percent at a strike of $100. Estimate by linear interpolation the implied volatility at a strike of $97, and state the type of smile.\nThe strike of $97 lies two fifths of the way from $95 to $100: \\(22-\\tfrac25\\times(22-19)=20.8\\) percent. Implied volatility falls as the strike rises, which is the skew typical of equity options."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises#34", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 34, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.exercises Exercises", "body": "15.3 A share that pays no dividend trades at $100, and the risk-free rate is 3 percent. The at-the-money implied volatility of six-month options is 20 percent, and that of options with a strike of $90 is 28 percent. Find the value of a six-month European put with a strike of $90 at each volatility. By what proportion would a dealer who used the at-the-money volatility undervalue the put?\nAt 20 percent: \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.1414\\); \\(d_1=[\\ln(100/90)+(0.03+0.02)\\times0.5]/0.1414=0.9218\\); \\(d_2=0.7804\\); \\(p=90e^{-0.015}\\times0.2176-100\\times0.1783=\\$1.46\\). At 28 percent: \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.1980\\); \\(d_1=[0.10536+(0.03+0.0392)\\times0.5]/0.1980=0.7069\\); \\(d_2=0.5089\\); \\(p=88.660\\times0.3054-100\\times0.2398=\\$3.10\\). The dealer would value the put at $1.46 against a market price of $3.10, an undervaluation of 53 percent.\n15.4 The volatility of a stock index is 16 percent per year. (a) Find the standard deviation of its daily change, taking 252 trading days in a year. (b) The index falls by 5 percent in one day. By how many standard deviations has it moved, and how often would Table 15.1 lead one to expect a change of at least that size?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises#35", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 35, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.exercises Exercises", "body": "(a) \\(16/\\sqrt{252}=1.008\\) percent. (b) \\(5/1.008=4.96\\) standard deviations. Under the normal distribution a change of more than 4 standard deviations is expected about once in 63 years and one of more than 5 about once in 6,900 years, so that a change of 4.96 should be seen roughly once in several thousand years. Falls of this size in major indices have occurred several times in a decade, which is the evidence for heavy tails.\n15.5 Six-month puts on a share with strikes of $78 and $82 trade at $2.10 and $3.30. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Estimate the risk-neutral probability that the share price is below $80 in six months.\nBy Result 15.2 with \\(\\delta=2\\): \\(e^{0.02}\\times(3.30-2.10)/4=1.0202\\times0.30=0.306\\), or about 31 percent.\n15.6 ★ Six-month calls on a share with strikes of $95, $100, and $105 trade at $7.90, $4.60, and $2.30. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Find the cost of the butterfly spread, the implied density at $100, and the approximate probability that the share price ends between $97.50 and $102.50."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises#36", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 36, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.exercises Exercises", "body": "The butterfly costs \\(7.90-2\\times4.60+2.30=\\$1.00\\). By Result 15.3, \\(g(100)=e^{0.02}\\times1.00/5^{2}=0.0408\\) per dollar. The probability of ending in a band of width $5 centred on $100 is approximately \\(0.0408\\times5=0.204\\), or about 20 percent.\n15.7 A share trades at $80. In two months the result of a clinical trial will move its price to either $92 or $70. The risk-free rate is 3 percent. (a) Find the values of two-month European calls and puts with a strike of $80, and verify put–call parity. (b) The implied volatilities of the calls with strikes of $72, $80, and $88 are 31.4, 41.9, and 34.7 percent. Describe the smile.\n(a) \\(p=(80e^{0.005}-70)/22=(80.401-70)/22=0.4728\\). Call: \\(e^{-0.005}\\times0.4728\\times12=\\$5.645\\). Put: \\(e^{-0.005}\\times0.5272\\times10=\\$5.246\\). Parity: \\(c-p=0.399\\) and \\(S_0-Ke^{-rT}=80-79.601=0.399\\). (b) Implied volatility is highest at the money and lower on both sides, a frown, which reflects a distribution with two peaks and thin tails beyond them."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises#37", "source_key": "fd-textbook:15.exercises", "chapter": 15, "section": "15.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 37, "title": "Chapter 15, Volatility Smiles and the Volatility Surface — 15.exercises Exercises", "body": "15.8 The VIX index stands at 24. (a) What standard deviation of the return on the S&P 500 over the next 30 days does this represent? (b) What daily standard deviation? (c) Explain why the VIX tends to rise on days when the S&P 500 falls.\n(a) \\(24\\times\\sqrt{30/365}=6.9\\) percent. (b) \\(24/\\sqrt{252}=1.51\\) percent. (c) The volatility of share prices tends to increase when prices fall, for the reasons given in Section 15.3, and the demand for puts as protection increases at the same time. Both raise the prices of index options and therefore the volatility that those prices imply."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
